Lucian's engineer at our studio today  we're going to talk about how the new  our studio one that one terminal feature  can help for our users who submit our  code to HPC and grids so for a bit of  background one of the ways that you can  scale R is by running our in parallel  across a grid computing environment  typically these environments have a  central resource manager like torque PBS  or LSF that handles the submission and  management of jobs and the typical  workflow for an R user is to develop our  code in our studio on their local  machine and then submit that our code to  the grid where it's run in parallel via  and typically a package like batch tools  or just a shell script that makes  requests to the the job management  platform again that would be something  like LSF PBS or torque so that's the the  standard setup and today we're going to  look at how the new terminal feature in  our studio 1.1 can make your life as an  analyst and are much easier when you're  using these types of HPC grids I'm going  to switch over to our studio this is our  studio version 1.1 you can see in  addition to the things I'll be showing  you today the new version 1.1 also has a  great dark theme that's that we're using  so I'm inside of a pretty simple project  in our studio and what I want to do is  take some code in this case it'll be a  hollow world example so really simple  code let me blow it up so everyone can  see and submit this code to run against  a cluster I'm using PBS in torque as my  job submission tool so the way that  you're able to do this easily today and  version 1.1 of our studio is with the  new terminal tab the terminal tab allows  you to open and close terminals on the  local machine  so I'll go ahead and get started with a  new terminal and this terminal gives you  full access to the the machine that is  currently running our studio so I can do  kind all kinds of things that I would  normally do in a terminal for instance I  can list the files in this project this  ternal tab though can make your life a  lot easier if you're trying to submit  jobs to an interactive cluster and we'll  look at a few tricks for doing that  today so the first thing that I'm going  to do after I've developed my code that  I want to run against a cluster is  interactively run that code once on one  edge node of my cluster and the way that  I'm going to do that specifically with  PBS and torque is I'm going to request  an interactive job the way I do that is  in the terminal I can run the command Q  sub - I and this asks the job scheduler  to give me an interactive terminal on  one of the nodes in the HPC cluster so  when I hit enter you can see there's two  things that are printed out the first is  this waiting for job 3 command this  tells me that PBS has received my  request and then the second line here  says job 3 is ready and that means that  the interactive terminal on that HPC  node is now ready and that's actually  where I am this terminal looks the same  but in fact I'm actually running now  instead of on the our studio machine in  one of the nodes of this PBS cluster so  to go back to our architecture diagram  I'm still inside of our studio on my  local machine but the terminal is now  pointed and running in a session on one  of the HPC nodes and this lets me do a  lot of interesting things one of them is  that I can actually run our on this edge  node so I'm going to do that just by  typing R and now I have an R console  inside of the  hpc grid node this can be really handy  because now I can take the script and  run it interactively in the same  environment where it'll ultimately be  executed in parallel and a headless  state so for instance I can put my  cursor here on the first line of the  file and by clicking command option  enter or control option enter on a  Windows machine I can send the our code  directly to this new console inside the  our studio terminal you can see there  were echoing hello PBS I can tell the  console is sleep for 15 seconds and I  can print goodbye so this is a really  useful feature and that I can walk  through my script exactly in the  environment where I will want it to run  and this is really handy for debugging  problems in HPC execution of your code  now one important thing to point out is  that this our console is entirely  separate from our studio and it can be  kind of easy to get confused here  so let's let's take a look inside of our  studio we have this console tab the  console tab is running our on the our  studio machine on this local machine  here and if I create a variable x equals  1 it'll show up inside of the  environment pane and I can see in my  history tab I also have that statement  so all of the features you would expect  in our studio if I switch over to the  terminal I do not have that same  connection between our studio and the  HAR console so I'm able to send code you  know line by line from this our file to  the terminal but that's it so for  example if I create a variable here Y is  1 that's not going to show up inside my  environment pain because I'm running in  this new detached mode inside my HPC  tool so it's not a 100% integration to  something we are working on at our  studio for the release 1.3  which will be in about a year but but it  is definitely a step in the right  direction and it's much easier than if  you've done this job submission worked  before all right so I'm going to go  ahead and quit out of our and I'm gonna  exit my interactive session you can see  when I typed exit there the job three  that I was in completed so now I'm back  in the terminal on the our studio local  machine now that I know my hello world  script runs my next step is to submit it  to the grid to run in parallel and I'm  going to do that using this submit job  shell script this is a simple example of  what a PBS submission script looks like  if you're not familiar with PBS  submission scripts that's okay  most companies that have an HPC system  in place have template files for you to  use but the setup is actually pretty  simple here we're saying that I want to  name this job hello underscore world I  want to submit it to the Q batch and  here we're specifying the resources for  this job  so I want one node and my wall time is  going to be two minutes so so a maximum  amount of time for this script would be  two minutes and then we tell the command  that we want to run in this case our  script hello world our and if that  command runs right we'll exit  successfully with a status code of zero  so a very simple submission script and  the way that we can run this from our  studio again making use of the new  terminal feature in the terminal I'm  going to run Q sub and then I'm just  going to give it the name of this script  when I do that and hit enter it's going  to print out a job ID and I can check on  the status of this job right from within  our studio by saying Q stat for and  it'll tell me some information about  this job if I want even more detailed  information I can say Q stat - f4 and  it'll print out a whole bunch of  information about this job  you can see that the the job was  actually running well I did this and  then if I do this again the job will  actually be done running so we can see  an exit status here of zero and also see  that output was routed directly back  into my home directory so here we have  the output of the hello world and that  standard error of the hello world script  for job four if we open this guy up you  can see what we expect that it says  hello PBS and goodbye so hopefully this  gives you a good sense for have a new  terminal tab can be used to both  interactively run our code inside your  HPC environment for debugging and for  testing as well as how easy it is to  submit jobs using the terminal to your  cluster and check on their status thanks  so much have a good day

